 "precalculus-vectors": "complex number|A number a + bi with i² = -1.|3 - 4i;modulus|The distance of a + bi from the origin.|the modulus of 3 - 4i is 5;vector|A quantity with magnitude and direction.|⟨3, -4⟩;component form|A vector written as its horizontal and vertical changes.|⟨5, 2⟩;magnitude|The length of a vector.|the magnitude of ⟨3, 4⟩ is 5;unit vector|A vector with magnitude 1.|⟨3/5, 4/5⟩;dot product|u·v = u₁v₁ + u₂v₂.|⟨1, 2⟩·⟨3, 4⟩ = 11;orthogonal|Perpendicular: the dot product is 0.|⟨1, 2⟩ and ⟨-2, 1⟩;projection|The component of one vector in the direction of another.|proj of ⟨2, 3⟩ onto ⟨1, 0⟩ is ⟨2, 0⟩;polar coordinates|A point written as (r, θ).|(2, π/3)",
 "precalculus-probability": "probability|The likelihood of an event, from 0 to 1.|P(heads) = 1/2;odds in favor|Favorable outcomes : unfavorable outcomes.|3 : 5;independent events|Events where one does not change the probability of the other.|Two coin flips;conditional probability|The probability of B given that A has occurred.|P(B given A) = P(A and B) ÷ P(A);permutation|An arrangement where order matters.|P(5, 2) = 20;combination|A selection where order does not matter.|C(5, 2) = 10;expected value|The long-run average value of a random variable.|E(X) = Σ x·P(x);normal distribution|A symmetric, bell-shaped distribution.|68% within 1 standard deviation;z-score|The number of standard deviations a value is from the mean.|z = (x - μ) ÷ σ;binomial probability|P(X = k) = C(n, k)pᵏ(1 - p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ.|Exactly 3 heads in 5 flips",
 "calculus-precalculus-review": "factor completely|Write an expression as a product that cannot be factored further.|x³ - 27 = (x - 3)(x² + 3x + 9);rational expression|A quotient of two polynomials.|(x² - 9)/(x - 3);excluded value|An input that makes a denominator zero.|x ≠ 3;exponential function|A function of the form a·bˣ.|f(x) = 2ˣ;natural logarithm|The logarithm with base e, written ln.|ln(e²) = 2;properties of logarithms|Rules for products, quotients, and powers inside logarithms.|ln(ab) = ln a + ln b;unit circle|A circle of radius 1 used to define sine and cosine.|cos(π/3) = 1/2;average rate of change|(f(b) - f(a)) ÷ (b - a).|Slope of a secant line;piecewise linear graph|A graph made of line segments.|A graph through (0, 1), (2, 3), (4, 0);domain|All input values for which a function is defined.|x ≥ 0 for √x;composite function|A function formed by applying one function to the output of another.|f(g(x));inverse function|A function that undoes another function.|ln x and eˣ",
 "calculus-limits-continuity": "limit|The value f(x) approaches as x approaches a.|lim (x → 2) x² = 4;one-sided limit|A limit from the left or from the right only.|lim (x → 0⁺) √x = 0;indeterminate form|An expression like 0 ÷ 0 that needs more work to evaluate.|(x² - 4)/(x - 2) at x = 2;limit at infinity|The value f(x) approaches as x grows without bound.|lim (x → ∞) 1/x = 0;infinite limit|f(x) grows without bound near a point.|lim (x → 0) 1/x² = ∞;continuous|lim (x → a) f(x) = f(a).|Polynomials are continuous everywhere;removable discontinuity|A hole: the limit exists but differs from f(a) or f(a) is undefined.|(x² - 1)/(x - 1) at x = 1;jump discontinuity|The one-sided limits exist but are different.|x ÷ abs(x) at x = 0;Intermediate Value Theorem|A continuous function on [a, b] takes every value between f(a) and f(b).|Guarantees a zero when signs change;Squeeze Theorem|If g ≤ f ≤ h and g, h share a limit, f has that limit too.|x² sin(1/x) → 0",
 "calculus-derivatives": "derivative|The instantaneous rate of change: lim (h → 0) (f(x + h) - f(x))/h.|(x²)′ = 2x;difference quotient|(f(x + h) - f(x)) ÷ h, the slope of a secant line.|For x²: 2x + h;tangent line|The line through a point with slope equal to the derivative there.|y - f(a) = f′(a)(x - a);power rule|(xⁿ)′ = nxⁿ⁻¹.|(x⁵)′ = 5x⁴;product rule|(uv)′ = u′v + uv′.|(x sin x)′ = sin x + x cos x;quotient rule|(u ÷ v)′ = (u′v - uv′) ÷ v².|Derivative of x/(x + 1);chain rule|(f(g(x)))′ = f′(g(x))·g′(x).|(sin(3x))′ = 3cos(3x);second derivative|The derivative of the derivative.|Acceleration is s″(t);differentiable|Has a derivative at a point: no corner, cusp, or break.|abs(x) is not differentiable at 0;instantaneous rate of change|The rate of change at a single instant: the derivative's value.|Speed at t = 3 seconds;secant line|A line through two points on a curve.|Slope is an average rate of change;implicit differentiation|Differentiating both sides of an equation without solving for y first.|x² + y² = 25",
 "calculus-applications-derivatives": "critical number|An x in the domain where f′ = 0 or f′ is undefined.|x = 1 for f = x³ - 3x;increasing|f′ > 0 on an interval.|x² is increasing on (0, ∞);relative extremum|A local maximum or minimum value.|f′ changes from + to - at a max;absolute extremum|The greatest or least value on an interval.|Check critical numbers and endpoints;concave up|f″ > 0: the graph bends upward.|x² is concave up;inflection point|Where concavity changes.|x = 0 for x³;related rates|Rates linked by differentiating an equation with respect to time.|A′ = 2πr·r′;optimization|Finding a maximum or minimum value in context.|Largest area with fixed perimeter;velocity|The derivative of position.|v(t) = s′(t);Mean Value Theorem|Some c has f′(c) = (f(b) - f(a)) ÷ (b - a).|Requires continuity and differentiability",
 "calculus-antiderivatives-definite-integrals": "antiderivative|A function F with F′ = f.|x³ is an antiderivative of 3x²;indefinite integral|The family of all antiderivatives, written with + C.|∫ 2x dx = x² + C;constant of integration|The arbitrary constant C in an indefinite integral.|x² + C;Riemann sum|A sum of rectangle areas approximating an integral.|Left, right, or midpoint sums;definite integral|The limit of Riemann sums: net signed area.|∫ from 0 to 2 x dx = 2;trapezoidal rule|Approximation using trapezoids instead of rectangles.|Width × average of the heights;net signed area|Area above the axis minus area below it.|Area below counts negative;additivity|∫ from a to b + ∫ from b to c = ∫ from a to c.|Split an integral at b;linearity of integrals|Constants factor out and sums split.|∫ (2f + g) = 2∫ f + ∫ g;integrand|The function being integrated.|x² in ∫ x² dx;limits of integration|The lower and upper bounds of a definite integral.|0 and 3 in ∫₀³ x dx;left Riemann sum|An approximation using the function's value at the left end of each subinterval.|Underestimates an increasing function",
 "calculus-fundamental-theorem-calculus": "Fundamental Theorem, Part 1|d/dx ∫ from a to x f(t) dt = f(x).|d/dx ∫ from 0 to x cos t dt = cos x;Fundamental Theorem, Part 2|∫ from a to b f(x) dx = F(b) - F(a).|∫ from 1 to 2 2x dx = 3;accumulation function|A function defined by an integral with a variable upper limit.|g(x) = ∫ from 0 to x f(t) dt;dummy variable|The integration variable, which does not appear in the result.|t in ∫ f(t) dt;net change|∫ from a to b f′(x) dx = f(b) - f(a).|Change in position from velocity;chain rule with integrals|d/dx ∫ from a to u(x) f(t) dt = f(u(x))u′(x).|d/dx ∫ from 0 to x² sin t dt;u-substitution|Reversing the chain rule by renaming an inner function.|u = x² + 1, du = 2x dx;evaluation notation|F(x) evaluated from a to b means F(b) - F(a).|x² from 1 to 3 is 8;definite integral|The net signed area between a curve and the x-axis on an interval.|∫₀² 3x dx = 6;antiderivative|A function whose derivative is the given function.|x³/3 for x²;total accumulation|The starting amount plus the integral of the rate of change.|Water in a tank after t minutes;Mean Value Theorem for Integrals|A continuous function equals its average value somewhere on the interval.|f(c)(b - a) = ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx",
